'''Nutrició del Món, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Nutrició del Món}}), commonly referred to as '''NDM''', is a Balboan {{w|fertilizer}} company and one of the largest producers and exporters of nitrogen fertilizers in the Adonian world. The company manufactures {{w|ammonia}}, {{w|urea}}, and {{w|NPK fertilizer|NPK blends}} at its production complex in [[Mansió de Turó]], a suburb of [[Sant Bart]] in [[Jala]] province, using {{w|natural gas}} supplied by [[CIEA]] as feedstock. It is the primary fertilizer supplier to the Abyalan agricultural sector and a major exporter to [[Lurandia]].

NDM is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: NDM) and is a constituent of the [[SCX 30]] index. [[CIEA]], the company's primary feedstock supplier, holds a 7% strategic stake in the company, reflecting the integrated nature of Balboa's petrochemical and agricultural sectors.

== History ==

=== Founding and state ownership (1971–1987) ===

Nutrició del Món was founded in 1971 as a state-owned enterprise during the Balboan government's post-war industrialization drive. The government recognized that Balboa's agricultural sector — and that of the broader Abyalan region — depended on imported fertilizers, primarily from Illypnian producers. At the same time, the expansion of the [[Badia Curta]] refinery complex under [[Petrolí de Balboa]] (later CIEA) was generating significant volumes of {{w|associated gas}} that could serve as feedstock for ammonia and urea production.

The first ammonia-urea plant was commissioned in 1974 at Mansió de Turó, then a small industrial suburb of Sant Bart, with a design capacity of 500,000 tonnes of urea per year. The plant used natural gas from PB's Badia Curta operations, establishing the integrated feedstock relationship that remains central to NDM's business model. The company was initially named '''Fertilitzants de Balboa''' and operated under the Ministry of Industry.

Throughout the 1970s and early 1980s, the company expanded its production capacity, adding a second ammonia plant in 1979 and a dedicated urea granulation facility in 1982. By 1985, NDM was producing over 1.5 million tonnes of urea annually, making it one of the largest nitrogen fertilizer producers in Abyala.

=== Privatization and expansion (1987–2005) ===

The Balboan economic reforms of the late 1980s led to the privatization of Fertilitzants de Balboa. In 1987, the company was reconstituted as a public limited company and renamed '''Nutrició del Món''' — "Nutrition of the World" — reflecting the government's ambition for the company to become a major exporter. An initial public offering in 1988 sold 60% of the company to private investors, with the government retaining a 25% stake and PB (by then transitioning toward privatization as CIEA) taking a 10% strategic stake.

The 1990s saw NDM expand its export markets significantly. The completion of the [[Balboa Canal]] expansion in 1995 and the modernization of Sant Cristòfor's port facilities enabled efficient shipment of bulk fertilizer to Lurandia, where agricultural demand was growing rapidly. NDM signed long-term supply agreements with Lurandian agricultural cooperatives and commodity traders, establishing itself as a reliable supplier to the Lurandian breadbasket region.

In 1998, NDM commissioned its first {{w|NPK fertilizer|NPK blending plant}}, allowing it to produce custom fertilizer blends tailored to specific soil conditions and crop requirements. This move into higher-value specialty fertilizers improved the company's margins and reduced its dependence on commodity urea prices.

=== Recent developments (2005–present) ===

The 2000s and 2010s saw continued expansion and modernization. NDM invested in plant efficiency upgrades, reducing its natural gas consumption per tonne of ammonia produced — a critical competitive advantage given Balboa's relatively high energy costs compared to major gas-producing regions. The company also expanded its product portfolio to include {{w|controlled-release fertilizer}}s and specialty micronutrient blends.

In 2018, CIEA increased its stake in NDM from 5% to 7%, formalizing a new long-term gas supply agreement that secured NDM's feedstock costs through 2035. The agreement gave NDM preferential access to associated gas from CIEA's Abyalan operations, reinforcing the integrated relationship between Balboa's oil and fertilizer industries.

Today, NDM operates one of the largest nitrogen fertilizer complexes in Abyala, with annual production capacity of 2.5 million tonnes of ammonia and 3.5 million tonnes of urea. The company supplies approximately 40% of Abyala's nitrogen fertilizer consumption and is one of the top five suppliers to the Lurandian market.

== Operations ==

NDM's operations are centred on its production complex in [[Mansió de Turó]], a suburb of [[Sant Bart]] in [[Jala]] province, which includes:

* '''Ammonia production''': Two {{w|Haber process|Haber-Bosch}} plants with combined capacity of 2.5 million tonnes per year, using natural gas from CIEA as feedstock
* '''Urea production''': Three urea synthesis plants with combined capacity of 3.5 million tonnes per year, producing both {{w|prilled urea|prilled}} and {{w|urea granules|granulated}} product
* '''NPK blending''': A dedicated blending facility producing custom fertilizer blends for specific crops and soil conditions
* '''Specialty products''': A smaller facility producing controlled-release fertilizers and micronutrient-enhanced blends

The company also operates a network of regional distribution centers across Abyala, providing storage and blending services closer to end markets.

== Markets ==

NDM's revenue is distributed across three primary markets:

* '''[[Abyala]]''' (~60%): The company is the primary nitrogen fertilizer supplier to the Abyalan agricultural sector, serving both large-scale commercial agriculture and smallholder farmers. Major customers include [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme]] and regional farming cooperatives.
* '''[[Lurandia]]''' (~35%): NDM is a major exporter to Lurandia, supplying fertilizer to the region's extensive grain and oilseed production areas. Exports are shipped through the Balboa Canal and Sant Cristòfor's bulk terminal facilities.
* '''Other''' (~5%): Smaller export markets in [[Sinchew]] and other regions.

The company's export logistics are facilitated by its proximity to the Balboa Canal and Sant Cristòfor's deepwater port, enabling efficient shipment to Lurandian ports on the Kendriyan Ocean.

== Relationship with CIEA ==

NDM's relationship with [[CIEA]] is fundamental to its business model. CIEA supplies natural gas to NDM under a long-term supply agreement that provides the fertilizer company with secure, competitively-priced feedstock. The agreement gives NDM access to associated gas from CIEA's Abyalan oil production operations, which would otherwise be flared or reinjected.

CIEA's 7% strategic stake in NDM, acquired in stages between 1988 and 2018, formalizes the relationship and ensures alignment between the two companies. The stake gives CIEA board representation and a degree of influence over NDM's strategic direction, while allowing NDM to remain an independent, publicly traded company.

The relationship is mutually beneficial: CIEA gains a reliable outlet for associated gas production and a strategic foothold in the agricultural supply chain, while NDM benefits from secure feedstock supply and favorable pricing. However, NDM's dependence on CIEA for feedstock makes it vulnerable to changes in CIEA's production levels and gas allocation policies.

== Ownership ==

NDM's ownership structure reflects its origins as a state-owned enterprise and its subsequent privatization:

* Public float: ~83%
* [[CIEA]]: 7% — strategic stake acquired 1988–2018
* [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]]: 6%
* [[Balboa Pension Fund]]: 4%

The company is professionally managed, with the CEO position held by [[Elena Puig i Torres]], a chemical engineer with extensive experience in the international fertilizer industry. The Chairman, [[Jordi Masdeu i Vidal]], represents the interests of long-term Balboan institutional shareholders.

== Regulatory and legal issues ==

=== Pollution inquisitions ===

NDM's production complex in [[Mansió de Turó]] has been subject to multiple investigations by Balboa's [[Inquisició de la Contaminació]] (Pollution Inquisition), a quasi-judicial body with authority to investigate environmental violations and impose sanctions. Ammonia and urea production involves significant environmental risks, including {{w|ammonia leak}}s, {{w|nitrogen oxide}} emissions, and {{w|eutrophication}} from nutrient discharge.

In 2014, the Inquisició de la Contaminació opened an investigation into NDM following a reported ammonia leak at the Mansió de turó facility that resulted in the hospitalisation of three workers and the evacuation of nearby residential areas. The investigation concluded that NDM had failed to maintain adequate safety systems and imposed a fine of ƒ12 million (approximately $3 million). NDM subsequently invested ƒ80 million in safety upgrades at the facility.

In 2019, the Inquisició launched a broader investigation into NDM's water discharge practices, following complaints from local farmers about contamination of irrigation water. The investigation was closed in 2021 without sanctions, after NDM demonstrated that its discharge met all regulatory standards and that the contamination originated from other sources.

=== AC Rural Pricing Litigation ===

In 2023, the [[Adonian Community]] filed a consumer protection action against NDM in the [[AC Tribunal of Areza]], alleging that the company had engaged in {{w|price gouging}} in rural communities in [[Lacashe]] and [[Tapuya]]. The case is docketed as ''[[Adonian Community v. Nutrició del Món]]''. The AC's complaint alleged that NDM had abused its dominant market position to charge excessive prices for nitrogen fertilizer in regions where smallholder farmers had no alternative suppliers.

The AC's case centred on NDM's pricing practices in the 2020–2022 period, during which global fertilizer prices surged following supply chain disruptions. The AC alleged that NDM had increased prices in Lacashe and Tapuya by 180–220 per cent over this period, compared to increases of 80–100 per cent in Balboa and Lurandia, and that this differential could not be explained by differences in transportation costs or local market conditions.

NDM contested the allegations, arguing that its pricing reflected higher distribution costs in remote rural markets, currency fluctuations, and the need to maintain supply security in volatile market conditions. The company also noted that it had maintained fertilizer supplies to Lacashe and Tapuya throughout the period when some competitors had withdrawn from these markets.

As of 2026, the case remains pending before the AC Tribunal. The litigation has drawn attention to the broader issue of Balboan corporate influence in Abyalan agricultural markets and has prompted calls for stronger AC-level regulation of fertilizer pricing.

